  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  A trough passage Saturday bring light precipitation to the\n   region. Fog is still a concern in the morning.\n\n-  Lull Sunday before the more substantial weather system arrives\n   by Monday with snow above 5,000 feet in central Idaho. \n\n-  Tuesday-Wednesday: 70% chance for a Yellowstone Low pressure system\n   that could bring wrap-around snow down to 2,000 to 4,000 feet \n   generally south of Interstate 90. Passes could be slick.\n\nFog redevelopment is another possibility later this evening as the\nvalleys of northwest Montana continue to be moist, though incoming\ncloud cover could prevent a more widespread event like we have\nbeen experiencing the last two nights.  \n\nRain will begin to fall later tonight across central Idaho as the\nnext trough nears the region. Due to its slower-moving and\nnegatively tilted nature, more precipitation will fall over \nIdaho...amounts ranging from 0.10 to 0.40\". Over western Montana,\nthe amounts will be much less, ranging from nothing up to several\nhundredths. Snow levels will generally be above 5500 feet. \n\nBy the time moisture arrives later Sunday into Monday morning, the\nsnow levels will range between 5000 to 6000 feet. The mild\nantecedent conditions combined with daytime incoming solar effects\ncould prevent much accumulation. Again, it appears Idaho will\nreceive the brunt of this system with precipitation totals ranging\nfrom 0.10 to 0.30\". In western Montana, totals range from a trace\nup to 0.20\". \n\nIt's the Tuesday into Wednesday time period where there could be a\nmore widespread snow event generally I-90 and south. The ensemble\nmeans depict an area of moderate lift with single model runs\nshowing a closed middle-level low system over the Yellowstone to\nsouthwest Montana area. The temperatures aloft are much colder\nthan they have been which could help drive down snow levels to\nsome valley floors in the heavier intensities. As for impacts, the\nmountain locations that could become slick at times include: Lost\nTrail, Lemhi Pass, Skalkaho Pass, Georgetown Lake, MacDonald Pass\nand Homestake Pass. Timing of the heavier precipitation will be\nkey with respect to impact level. If much of it falls during the\nday Tuesday, then melting will offset accumulations. Right now the\nthere is quite a lot of spread amongst the ensemble members with\nregards to precipitation. For now, there is a 75% chance for at\nleast a trace to several of inches of snow from Lemhi County to \nsouthwest Montana.",
